          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Eleanor Roosevelt</strong><br>\nEleanor Roosevelt was born on October 11, 1884, in New York City. She was the niece of President Theodore Roosevelt and lost her parents at a young age. Raised by relatives, Eleanor attended a girls' school in England led by the feminist Marie Souvestre, who strongly influenced her thinking. Her experiences with loss and education shaped Eleanor's commitment to social justice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marriage and Political Partnership</strong><br>\nIn 1915, she married her cousin Franklin D. Roosevelt, who later became the U.S. President. As First Lady from 1933 to 1945, she fundamentally transformed the role. She held regular press conferences and wrote a daily column addressing social issues. Although their marriage was also a political partnership, they had differing views on some matters.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Advocacy for the Poor and Disadvantaged</strong><br>\nDuring the Great Depression, Eleanor Roosevelt passionately advocated for the poor and unemployed. She visited workers, farmers, and citizens to understand their living conditions and promote their concerns. Eleanor used her influence to support and expand the New Deal, her husband's program for economic recovery.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "es"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
