            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Fight for Civil and Women's Rights</strong><br>\nEleanor Roosevelt was an tireless advocate for the civil rights of African Americans and gender equality. She opposed racial segregation and championed equality in the workplace. She also advocated for women's rights by encouraging women to pursue careers and political offices.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Human Rights</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Eleanor Roosevelt supported the war efforts and emphasized the importance of human rights. She visited soldiers domestically and abroad, serving as a crucial ambassador for American ideals. Her vision of a world based on the respect for human rights was groundbreaking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War: Work at the United Nations</strong><br>\nAfter her husband's death in 1945, Eleanor Roosevelt was appointed as a delegate to the United Nations. There, she advocated for global peace and the establishment of an international community. She played a key role in drafting the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "es"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
