          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Chair of the UN Human Rights Committee</strong><br>\nAs the Chair of the UN Human Rights Committee, Eleanor Roosevelt tirelessly worked for the adoption of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in 1948. This document continues to set global standards for the freedom and dignity of individuals and is considered her greatest legacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Eleanor Roosevelt's Public Engagements and Lectures</strong><br>\nEleanor Roosevelt was a sought-after speaker and published several books about her experiences and beliefs. She spoke about democracy, social justice, and the role of the United Nations. Her influence also extended to promoting education and youth programs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Commemoration</strong><br>\nEleanor Roosevelt passed away on November 7, 1962. She left a powerful legacy as an activist, diplomat, and a woman who used her position to fight for a better world. Today, numerous educational programs, awards, and institutions commemorate her contributions to society and human rights.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
